PingPull (Securelist MD5: 3f2b…) is written in Rust with quiche DoH stack.
1) Dynamic Analysis: Frida script injects into curl!quiche_conn_handshake():
   Interceptor.attach(Module.findExportByName('libquiche.so', 'quiche_conn_handshake'), {
      onEnter: function(args) {
         send("DoH packet at: " + args[0]);
      },
      onLeave: function(retval) { }
   });
   Hook SSL_CTX_new() in libssl.so to dump the ESNI keys:
   frida -p $(pidof pingpull) -l doh_trace.js --runtime=v8
   After hook, inspect QUIC long header: 8-byte connection ID + 4-byte packet number; the encrypted DoH payload starts at offset 0x25 with EDNS OPT RR containing bot ID as 16-byte chunk (type 0x1234).
2) Static Unpack: In x64dbg set breakpoint on kernel32!WriteFile after decrypting the payload; in dump window read from RCX + 0x20 to 0x30; the next 2 bytes are the EDNS length (0x0012) followed by the 16-byte bot ID (xor 0xdead with key found in .rodata).
Mitigation: Block known DoH resolvers via DNS sinkhole (1.1.1.1 → 0.0.0.0); enable Windows DNS-over-HTTPS GPO with enterprise root CA validation; use Suricata rule:
   alert udp any 53 -> any any (msg:"DNSDoH Quic handshake"; content:"|c0 00 00 00 01|"; depth:5; sid:20236001; rev:1;)
Actor: Lazarus Group 2023 TA428 campaign against Russian defense contractors.

